.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt BIO_PUSH 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BIO_push ,
.Nm BIO_pop ,
.Nm BIO_set_next
.Nd add and remove BIOs from a chain
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/bio.h
.Ft BIO *
.Fo BIO_push
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "BIO *next"
.Fc
.Ft BIO *
.Fo BIO_pop
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo BIO_set_next
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "BIO *next"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn BIO_push
pushes
.Fa b
on
.Fa next .
If
.Fa b
is NULL the function does nothing and returns
.Fa next .
Otherwise it prepends
.Fa b ,
which may be a single BIO or a chain of BIOs, to
.Fa next
(unless
.Fa next
is NULL). It then makes a control call on
.Fa b
and returns
.Fa b .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_pop
removes the BIO
.Fa b
from any chain is is part of.
If
.Fa b
is NULL the function does nothing and returns NULL. Otherwise it makes a
control call on
.Fa b
and returns the next BIO in the chain, or NULL if there is no next BIO.
The removed BIO becomes a single BIO with no association with the
original chain, it can thus be freed or be made part of a different
chain.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_set_next
replaces the existing next BIO in a chain with the BIO pointed to by
.Fa next .
The new chain may include some of the same BIOs from the old chain or it
may be completely different.
.Sh NOTES
The names of these functions are perhaps a little misleading.
.Fn BIO_push
joins two BIO chains whereas
.Fn BIO_pop
deletes a single BIO from a chain, the deleted BIO does not need to be
at the end of a chain.
.Pp
The process of calling
.Fn BIO_push
and
.Fn BIO_pop
on a BIO may have additional consequences (a control call is made to the
affected BIOs).
Any effects will be noted in the descriptions of individual BIOs.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn BIO_push
returns the head of the chain, which usually is
.Fa b ,
or
.Fa next
if
.Fa b
is NULL.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_pop
returns the next BIO in the chain, or NULL if there is no next BIO.
.Sh EXAMPLES
For these examples suppose
.Em md1
and
.Em md2
are digest BIOs,
.Em b64
is a base64 BIO and
.Em f
is a file BIO.
.Pp
If the call:
.Bd -literal
 BIO_push(b64, f);
.Ed
.Pp
is made then the new chain will be
.Em b64-f .
After making the calls
.Bd -literal
 BIO_push(md2, b64);
 BIO_push(md1, md2);
.Ed
.Pp
the new chain is
.Em md1-md2-b64-f .
Data written to
.Em md1
will be digested by
.Em md1
and
.Em md2 ,
base64 encoded, and finally written to
.Em f .
.Pp
It should be noted that reading causes data to pass in the reverse
direction, that is data is read from
.Em f ,
base64 decoded, and digested by
.Em md2
and then
.Em md1 .
.Pp
The call:
.Bd -literal
 BIO_pop(md2);
.Ed
.Pp
will return
.Em b64
and the new chain will be
.Em md1-b64-f .
Data can be written to and read from
.Em md1
as before, except that
.Em md2
will no more be applied.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bio 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn BIO_set_next
function was added in OpenSSL 1.1.0.
